# Little Adventure World

Pair with [visual-design.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/visual-design.mdc) (Soft Adventure tokens and shape language), [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc) (canonical character), [child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc), [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc), [sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc), [little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc), [little-adventure-audio.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-audio.mdc), and [architecture.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc).

This document defines **reusable visual and design rules** for gradually enriching child-facing screens with world context. It is **not** a scene engine, asset registry, or CMS. Individual environment **artwork** specs may live in `docs/design/` when product defines them.

**Visual coherence is more important than implementation convenience.** Choose the representation that best preserves the approved illustrated language and child-facing quality while keeping architecture simple.

---

## 1. Core principle

The child experience is composed of:

**CHARACTER + ILLUSTRATED ENVIRONMENT + MEANINGFUL PROPS + INTERACTION LAYER + EXISTING LEARNING UI**

(Plus day / real-life context, gentle interaction, and subtle motion where appropriate.)

The environment helps the child understand **where** the activity happens and **what** is going on. It must **not** become decorative noise.

Without reading instructions, a well-composed scene should answer:

- **Where am I?**
- **What is happening?**
- **What can I touch?**

**Learning clarity comes first.** World-building supports recognition and warmth; it must not compete with the task, choices, drag targets, or celebration hierarchy.

---

## 2. Character (unchanged identity)

**`LittleAdventureCharacter`** remains the **only** child-facing character. See [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc).

The world system must **never**:

- Create a separate mascot or scene-specific character
- Change clothing, backpack, proportions, or visual identity per scene
- Fork per-game companion art

The **environment changes around the character**; the character does not become a different person for School, Chai Shop, Cake, Park, etc.

Within a scene, the character may:

- Use the existing **mood** system
- **Respond** to interaction (presentation props from screens/hooks — not logic inside the character component)
- **Move subtly** within the composition (placement, gentle motion via existing shell/motion patterns)

Character implementation boundaries (no game logic, audio, or progression inside the character component) stay as defined in the character rule.

---

## 3. Environment language

Environments should feel like **soft children’s picture-book illustrations** — **the same illustration family as production character art**, not geometric CSS/SVG scenery behind a polished character cutout.

**Characteristics:** warm, simple, rounded, tactile, friendly, visually clear, layered but not busy, soft depth, limited detail, consistent perspective, consistent scale, consistent lighting, **shared softness and colour harmony with the character**.

**Avoid:** photorealism, detailed realistic backgrounds, busy scenes, excessive gradients, neon colours, excessive decorative objects, visual clutter, classroom/corporate UI aesthetics, overly babyish illustrations, **flat icon-style buildings/paths used as the primary environment read**.

### Illustrated environment assets (first-class)

When a scene needs a **cohesive illustrated place**, the environment **may** be a **dedicated environment illustration asset** (e.g. raster WebP/PNG), not only CSS/SVG primitives.

- The asset represents **environment only** (sky, ground, buildings, vegetation, path, ambient depth).
- It must **not** include the canonical character, application UI, learning cards, labels, buttons, audio controls, navigation, or celebration elements.
- It must **not** be a giant flattened screenshot of the whole screen.
- The canonical character remains a **separate** production asset composited above (see [little-adventure-character.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-character.mdc)).

**CSS/SVG** remain valid for **simple supporting elements** (page blend, minor accents, non-scene UI) but must **not** substitute for cohesive illustration when they **materially reduce** visual quality or coherence with the character.

Environment artwork should be specified with **character placement, scale, ground contact, and perspective** in mind — not designed as wallpaper the character is pasted onto.

### Scene layers (composition model)

Compose screens with a consistent **back → front** stack:

| Layer | Role |
|-------|------|
| **1. Environment** | Illustrated world / background (asset and/or minimal supporting markup) |
| **2. Character** | Canonical **`LittleAdventureCharacter`** production artwork |
| **3. Interaction objects** | Optional scene-specific tappable props (present or future) |
| **4. Existing UI** | Learning, progression, TODAY, weekday strip, navigation, audio — React/UI |

Within the environment illustration, use **background → midground → ground → subtle foreground** depth (see §4). Layers relate spatially with **one coherent perspective** and **one scale system**. The character sits **in** the world (see §6), not on an unrelated flat panel.

Do not prescribe exact z-index or component names here.

---

## 4. Environment depth

**Background:** distant, soft, low-detail, visually quiet.

**Midground:** primary environmental read (“this is a school,” “this is a shop”) — simplified shapes, clear silhouettes.

**Foreground:** interaction-ready objects — visually distinct, larger relative scale, enough contrast to discover. Foreground must **never** obscure the character or the **primary learning / interaction target**.

Depth cues: soft overlap, gentle size falloff, light atmospheric quiet in the back — not stacked drop shadows, glass stacks, or harsh 3D realism (see [visual-design.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/visual-design.mdc)).

---

## 5. Props

Every prop should have a **purpose**. Prefer **few meaningful props** over many decorations.

| Category | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| **Context prop** | Establishes where the child is |
| **Interaction prop** | Something the child can touch or manipulate |
| **Learning prop** | Directly supports the learning objective |
| **Decorative prop** | Only when it strengthens the scene **without** clutter |

Props use the same **Soft Adventure illustration language** as the character: rounded forms, clear silhouettes, simple colour blocks, limited detail, consistent perspective, child-friendly scale.

Do **not** define prop libraries, registries, or shared prop components until implementation is explicitly requested.

---

## 6. Character ↔ environment relationship

The character should feel **inside** the world, not pasted on a wallpaper.

**Rules of thumb:**

- **Ground placement:** feet (or logical base) align with the scene ground plane; no floating unless intentionally playful and still readable.
- **Scale:** character scale stays consistent across scenes relative to doors, counters, trees, etc.
- **Overlap:** midground may partially overlap character edges only when it aids depth; never hide face, hands needed for mood, or primary gesture.
- **Depth:** character reads in front of midground context unless a deliberate foreground prop frames the action — foreground still must not block the learning target.
- **Grounding:** optional soft shadow or ground contact — light, consistent, not heavy UI elevation.
- **Spacing:** leave clear air around the character; scenery must not crowd or overpower.
- **Environmental touch:** character may orient toward a prop or context, via placement and mood — not via alternate costumes or duplicate figures.

The character remains one of the **strongest focal points**; scenery must not visually overpower him.

---

## 7. Context (day / real-life cues)

Scenes communicate **real-world context** through visual cues, not long copy.

**Examples only** (do not implement these scenes in rules or code until requested):

| Context | Possible cues |
|---------|----------------|
| Monday → School | school building, entrance, sign, trees, path, backpack-adjacent context |
| Friday → Chai Shop | small shop, counter, cups, sign, simple street |
| Saturday → Cake | cake, table, simple home/celebration context |
| Sunday → Park | trees, grass, path, simple play element |

Product may add other days/events; cues stay **simple and readable** on tablet.

Context data belongs in **content/config** when implemented ([architecture.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc)), not hardcoded literals scattered in UI.

---

## 8. Interaction in the world

Interaction should feel like **exploring the world**, not only answering a quiz.

For tappable world objects:

- Subtle **scale / pop** feedback; gentle movement allowed
- Use **centralized audio** for meaningful feedback where appropriate ([little-adventure-audio.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-audio.mdc))
- Follow [sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc) and [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc): no flashing, aggressive bounce, red error states, or “wrong” treatment
- **Reward curiosity** when optional taps are intentional — pleasant response even outside a formal question

**Do not** add arbitrary taps just to make a scene “interactive.” Every interaction needs a **clear purpose** (context, learning, or deliberate delight aligned with the activity).

Formal challenge interactions (correct/incorrect, celebration, Next) remain governed by existing game, UI, and [little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc) rules — world rules do not change that behavior.

---

## 9. Motion in the world

Motion makes the world feel **alive**, not distracting.

**Allowed (examples):** gentle tree sway, subtle character breathing/float (existing patterns), small prop motion, soft floating elements, tiny environmental movement, character reacting to touch.

**Avoid:** constant movement everywhere, rapid animation, flashing, looping high-energy motion, parallax that overloads, many objects moving at once.

Honor **`prefers-reduced-motion`**: essential state stays visible without relying on ambient loops ([sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc)).

Ambient world motion is **decoration with restraint**; play-loop motion stays **purposeful** (feedback, state, celebration).

---

## 10. Colour system

**Foundation palette** (harmonize new environmental colours with these — do not replace with per-scene palettes):

| Token role | Hex (reference) |
|------------|-----------------|
| Warm canvas / light background | `#FFF9F0` |
| Sunny accent | `#FFD95A` |
| Sky / calm blue | `#8DD8F7` |
| Soft green | `#A8DFA8` |
| Warm orange | `#FFB36B` |
| Readable neutral (text/UI chrome where needed) | `#374151` |

In implementation, prefer **project design tokens** over raw hex in components ([visual-design.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/visual-design.mdc)). Additional hues for props and environments must **harmonize** with this set.

---

## 11. Tablet-first composition

**Primary:** tablet **portrait**. **Secondary:** landscape.

**Rules (no fixed pixel layouts yet):**

- **Safe margins** — keep interactive props and primary targets away from screen edges and system gestures
- **Character placement** — stable, readable zone; not hidden under chrome or foreground clutter
- **Interaction zones** — foreground props and challenge targets in thumb-friendly regions with ≥ **60px** effective touch where tappable ([child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc))
- **Navigation** — do not overlap world art with back, audio, or other persistent controls; reserve clear space
- **Hierarchy preserved** — world art fills space without turning the screen into a “find the button” puzzle

---

## 12. Visual hierarchy

Default stacking of attention:

1. **Child’s action / interaction target** (learning or primary tap)
2. **`LittleAdventureCharacter`**
3. **Important environmental context** (where / what)
4. **Supporting props**
5. **Decorative details**

The scene must never become wallpaper where the child searches for what matters.

During **celebration**, hierarchy follows [little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-celebration-progression.mdc) (Good Job → character → effects); world layers should **support**, not fight, that composition when both are visible.

---

## 13. Reusability without a scene engine

Future places (School, Chai Shop, Cake, Park, etc.) should share this **visual language** and **art direction** — perspective, depth, lighting, scale, ground treatment, organic shapes, character/environment relationship — not reinvent a new style per day.

**Do not** (unless explicitly requested):

- Build a generic scene engine, world engine, or heavy visual framework
- Introduce complex scene schemas, CMS, or large visual registries
- Premature shared abstractions with only one consumer

Supporting illustrated environments is **simple**: static assets + screen-level composition ([architecture.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/architecture.mdc)). Add narrow reuse when a **second real scene** repeats the same pattern.

## 13A. Visual review

For **major child-facing visual changes** (new environment artwork, scene composition swaps, character/environment integration), **review the actual rendered screen** on a tablet target (portrait first) before considering the work done. Coherence between character and environment is a acceptance criterion.

---

## 14. Accessibility / sensory friendliness

All [sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc) and [child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc) principles apply to world layers.

**No:** flashing, sudden visual changes, harsh transitions, excessive simultaneous motion, noisy decorative effects, visual overload.

Important information (targets, character, context) stays **visually clear**; do not rely on colour alone for state ([sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/sensory-friendly-interaction.mdc)).

---

## 15. Relationship to existing product layers

Little Adventure World is a **visual layer** that gradually enriches the experience. It does **not** replace the product’s core systems.

**Remain intact unless explicitly changed elsewhere:**

- Typography and Soft Adventure child UI patterns
- Navigation and routing
- Centralized audio
- Session engine and challenge logic
- Buttons and child UI components
- Celebration and child-guided progression
- **`LittleAdventureCharacter`** architecture (shell vs figure)

World art **complements** existing UI; the app must not feel like a different product overnight.

---

## Documented artwork specs

Product-defined **environment artwork** specifications (e.g. Monday → School) live under `docs/design/` when created. Implementation follows those specs plus this rule; do not contradict celebration, audio, or character boundaries.

## Intentionally unspecified (until product requests)

- Component APIs, folder structure, and z-index implementation for each screen
- Exact breakpoints beyond tablet-first principles
- Visual asset registry or CMS
- Optional ambient interactions per scene
- Export pipeline and illustration tooling

When implementation begins, read this rule together with the paired rules above and any relevant `docs/design/` spec.
